Lists in Python
    People use lists all the time. You might have a to-do list, a wish list, a music playlist, or even a bucket list. 
    Lists are incredibly useful, and they are one of the most common tools in coding too.

    When you first learned about strings, you might have tried to make a "list" by joining multiple words together with the \n newline character:

    Example:
        shopping_list = "Eggs" + "\n" + "Tomatoes" + "\n" + "Milk"

    This code gives you a single string that displays nicely on the screen when printed, but there’s not much else you can do with it. 
    Think of other things you might want to do with a real list:

        How could you perform an action on each member of the list without needing to retype them all?

        How could you double-check whether you've already added something to the list?

        If your list has grown to hundreds of items, could you easily remove the one that says “Tomatoes”?

        What if your list isn't made of strings, but something else—like a list of prices that you'd like to add up?

        What's the first thing? The last thing? The 24th thing?

        How many things are there in total?

        How can you rearrange or sort the list?

    In Python, this is solved by a type simply called a list.

    A list is an important data abstraction—a way to think about a collection of items as a single unit without having to worry about how they are stored individually. 
    Lists, like all data abstractions, help you simplify your code by providing built-in methods to solve all the dilemmas above.

    Think of any app or website you’ve ever used where you’ve scrolled through a feed or a set of search results. 
    That program is almost certainly using a list.

    In this lesson, you’ll learn about creating and working with Python lists.